/-
# Non-normal maximal subgroups of finite index in branch groups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 20.101)

Kourovka Notebook, Problem 20.101 (A. Thillaisundaram), marked solved in the 21st issue: let `G` be
a finitely generated branch group. Are all finite-index maximal subgroups of `G` necessarily normal?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Finiteness
public import Mathlib.Order.Atoms
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.BranchGroupShared

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.BranchGroupMaximalSubgroupsNormalKourovka20Problem101Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 20.101 (A. Thillaisundaram). UnsolvedMath
record UM-00701759 (`KOU-20.101`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

A branch group acts faithfully and level-transitively on a locally finite rooted tree in which every
vertex has at least two children, with every rigid level stabilizer (the subgroup generated by the
rigid stabilizers of the vertices of a level) of finite index: the definition of
Garciarena-Petschick (Section 2) for subgroups of `Aut(X*)`. The definitions are in the shared
module `WantedExt.GroupTheory.BranchGroupShared`, which repeats those of the OpenConjectures entry
`BranchGroupsBoundedlyAcyclic` (Kourovka 21.116); WantedExt cannot import OpenConjectures.
-/

open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.BranchGroupShared

/-- Kourovka 20.101 (A. Thillaisundaram): in a finitely generated branch group every maximal
subgroup of finite index is normal. -/
def branchGroupFiniteIndexMaximalSubgroupsNormal : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G], Group.FG G → IsBranchGroup G →
    ∀ M : Subgroup G, IsCoatom M → M.FiniteIndex → M.Normal

/--
Resolved false: the Kourovka Notebook (21st issue) answers 20.101 "No, not necessarily" (P. Neumann,
1986), and Garciarena and Petschick (2024, Theorem 4.1) construct a finitely generated branch group
all of whose maximal subgroups have finite index, one of which is not normal. Source: E. I. Khukhro
and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue,
arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 20.101 (A. Thillaisundaram); resolution: M. E. Garciarena and J.
M. Petschick, Maximal subgroups in torsion branch groups, preprint, arXiv:2410.06783 (2024); P. M.
Neumann, Illinois J. Math. 30 (1986), 301-316, Garciarena-Petschick, Theorem 4.1: there exists a
finitely generated branch group that is in MF (all maximal subgroups of finite index) but not in MN
(all maximal subgroups normal); the Kourovka Notebook (21st issue) marks 20.101 solved, "No, not
necessarily" (P. Neumann), https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.06783.
-/
public theorem_wanted branchGroupFiniteIndexMaximalSubgroupsNormal_refuted :
    ¬ branchGroupFiniteIndexMaximalSubgroupsNormal

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.BranchGroupMaximalSubgroupsNormalKourovka20Problem101Wanted
